FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/runtime.rs:1979 — The trusted-object shortcut accepts any `Value::Object` named `deque` carrying `kind: "deque"`, sets `trusted = true`, and therefore skips `validate_stack_tree` (src/runtime.rs:2026-2028). The report/SPEC justify this with "source cannot construct an Object", but the language has `object` declarations (`src/interpreter.rs:2919 declare_object`, which collects the declaration's `has` fields at `src/interpreter.rs:2945`). A source-declared object named `deque` with `has kind`, `has front`, `has back` therefore appears able to reach the trusted path; `stack_cons` then validates only the top tree nodes (src/runtime.rs:2250-2256), so a malformed descendant is accepted and only fails on a later pop. I could NOT verify the exact instance-construction path within the tool budget — settle it by running an `object deque` declaration with a forged `front` tree through `collections.push_left`/`pop_left`. Fix: key trust on a private marker a `Statement::Object` cannot produce (or validate object trees too), and add a test for a source-declared `object deque`.
2. [MINOR] src/runtime.rs:2042 (via src/value.rs:425-430 and src/runtime.rs:1733-1742) — Logical deque equality and hashing materialise both whole deques: `deques_equal` calls `deque_logical_items` twice, each of which runs `stack_pop_order` and allocates a `Vec<Value>` of every item. A 1,000,000-item deque therefore allocates two ~1M-element vectors on a single `is`/`==`, and Counter/defaultdict/groupby pay the same cost per key. It is bounded, but it directly undercuts the phase's claim that module deques no longer walk their nodes per operation. Fix: compare/hash the spines incrementally, or document the cost and pin it with a cost test.
3. [MINOR] tests/collections_test.rs:220 — The new equality coverage builds the second deque only with `push_right`. There is no test for `push_left`, mixed-end construction, or a deque whose split changed via pops/`deque_rebalance`, so the SPEC claim "compare equal ... however their ends are split" is unpinned on exactly the rebalancing paths where the order arithmetic is easiest to get wrong. Fix: assert equality across `deque(list)`, a `push_left`-built deque, and a deque re-split by alternating pops.
4. [MINOR] phases/phase-099/REPORT.md:22 — The evidence prose is stale: it says "the 38-test binary still finishes in seconds" while the table records 43 tests, and the 285 s / 4.2 s / "seconds" figures are not tied to a named build. Fix: restate the final numbers for the current 43-test binary.

